{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To determine the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) for which the system of equations has infinitely many solutions, we need to ensure that the two equations are dependent, meaning one equation is a scalar multiple of the other. The given system is:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3ax + 3y = 5b, \\\\\n2x + y = 5.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we express the second equation in a form similar to the first equation:\n\\[\n2x + y = 5 \\implies 2x + y - 5 = 0.\n\\]\nWe need to find a way to make the coefficients of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in the first equation proportional to those in the second equation. Let's rewrite the first equation:\n\\[\n3ax + 3y = 5b \\implies 3ax + 3y - 5b = 0.\n\\]\nFor the two equations to be dependent, the coefficients of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) must be proportional. That is, there must exist a constant \\(k\\) such that:\n\\[\n3a = 2k \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3 = k.\n\\]\nFrom \\(3 = k\\), we get \\(k = 3\\). Substituting \\(k = 3\\) into the first equation, we get:\n\\[\n3a = 2 \\cdot 3 \\implies 3a = 6 \\implies a = 2.\n\\]\nNow, substituting \\(k = 3\\) into the constant term, we get:\n\\[\n5b = 3 \\cdot 5 \\implies 5b = 15 \\implies b = 3.\n\\]\nThus, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) that allows the system to have infinitely many solutions is \\((2, 3)\\).\n\n","prefix_end":1198,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,8253,279,11457,6716,1124,1188,64,11,293,10699,8,369,892,279,1849,315,37906,702,71500,1657,9904,11,582,1184,311,5978,429,279,1378,37906,525,17749,11,7290,825,23606,374,264,17274,5248,315,279,1008,13,576,2661,1849,374,510,59,9640,59,7265,90,23910,532,18,706,488,220,18,88,284,220,20,65,11,90155,17,87,488,379,284,220,20,624,59,408,90,23910,532,59,2533,5338,11,582,3158,279,2086,23606,304,264,1352,4428,311,279,1156,23606,510,59,9640,17,87,488,379,284,220,20,1124,6383,550,220,17,87,488,379,481,220,20,284,2