,17,87,43010,5,28,20,624,59,408,90,6612,9,92,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFor what ordered pair $(a,b)$ are there infinite solutions $(x,y)$ to the system \\begin{align*}\n3ax+3y&=5b,\\\\\n2x+y&=5.\n\\end{align*}","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"d41822945b2cb86d4089d4dce084695e91dad83dc012992d6fed05559eb9944d"},"seconds":13.616138696670532,"seed":558666539580339600,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) for which the system of equations has infinitely many solutions, we need to ensure that the two equations are dependent, meaning one equation is a scalar multiple of the other. The given system is:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3ax + 3y = 5b, \\\\\n2x + y = 5.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we express the second equation in a form similar to the first equation:\n\\[\n2x + y = 5 \\implies 2x + y - 5 = 0.\n\\]\nWe need to find a way to make the coefficients of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in the first equation proportional to those in the second equation. Let's rewrite the first equation:\n\\[\n3ax + 3y = 5b \\implies 3ax + 3y - 5b = 0.\n\\]\nFor the two equations to be dependent, the coefficients of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) must be proportional. That is, there must exist a constant \\(k\\) such that:\n\\[\n3a = 2k \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3 = k.\n\\]\nFrom \\(3 = k\\), we get \\(k = 3\\). Substituting \\(k = 3\\) into the first equation, we get:\n\\[\n3a = 2 \\cdot 3 \\implies 3a = 6 \\implies a = 2.\n\\]\nNow, substituting \\(k = 3\\) into the constant term, we get:\n\\[\n5b = 3 \\cdot 5 \\implies 5b = 15 \\implies b = 3.\n\\]\nThus, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) that allows the system to have infinitely many solutions is \\((2, 3)\\).\n\nTo verify, we substitute \\(a = 2\\) and \\(b = 3\\) back into the original equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3(2)x + 3y = 5(3) \\implies 6x + 3y = 15, \\\\\n2x + y = 5.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nWe can see that the first equation is indeed a multiple of the second equation:\n\\[\n6x + 3y = 3(2x + y) = 3 \\cdot 5 = 15.\n\\]\nTherefore, the system is dependent and has infinitely many solutions.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{(2, 3)}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(2, 3)}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:846"}
